eculation for  thousands of years  but when anthony van leeuwenhoek first  peered through his lenses in the 17th  century  he opened a door to another hidden side  of earth's biosphere  the microscopic world  the denizens of this world may be tiny  but their collective might  is huge there are microscopic animals  like rotifers and tardigrades  but other creatures consisting of only  one cell cannot rightly be considered  animal plant or fungus  those single cells with a nucleus are  the protists  which include predatory amoeba as well  as multitudinous forms of  photosynthesizing  algae in shades of green brown and gold  seaweeds including the great kelp  forests of the world's coastlines  a little more than a loose collection of  these sun-loving  single cells  but even the protists are outnumbered by  an even  smaller even more numerous and  potentially more diverse group  the bacteria  these single cells lack a nucleus or  other cellular machinery  but that hasn't stopped them from  conquering the entire biosphere  just a thousandth of a millimeter across  these hardy prokaryotic cells  permeate every possible habitat on earth  they've been found deep beneath ice  sheets clustering around acidic  volcano pools and floating high in the  atmosphere  they are on and in our bodies in  astounding number  it is a commonly voiced myth that  bacterial cells outnumber our own body's  cells  some ten to one while modern scientists  have since revised that figure down  to roughly one to one each of us still  plays host  to 30 trillion other living things  [Music]  despite their miniscule size the sum  total of bacterial biomass  is some thousand times greater than that  of all humans on earth  as we go about our long lives learning  about the natural world and developing  the tools and technology with which to  understand it  it's easy for us human animals to  consider ourselves  masters of all  we undoubtedly have abilities no other  living things possess  but elsewhere within the biosphere other  life forms have skills and natural  talents of which we can only dream  [Music]  we cannot harvest and carry a hundred  times our own weight  like the industrious ants we cannot make  our own food from the raw  energy of the sun like plants we cannot  eke out in existence in caustic pools of  acid  like the hardiest prokaryote  [Music]  the diversity of earth's modern  biosphere is one of its greatest wonders  and we about minor players in its  spectacular chorus line  by every measure we humans are outgunned  and outnumbered  but at the same time we are part of this  vast  interconnected web of life able to trace  our origins back  some 4 billion years  and reconstructing the family tree of  earth's living world  can help us find our true place within  our planet's  unique biosphere  for centuries scholars have tried to  understand our place within the natural  world  in ancient greece aristotle was among  the first to speculate that animals  were superior to the plants because of  their ability to move  and to sense and react to the outside  world  classical teachings like these persisted  into the middle ages  when a more elaborate scheme of  classification was developed  that ranked all the matter in life that  we were aware of as part of a great  chain of being  at the pinnacle of this great chain was  of course god  the creator of all followed closely  behind by the angels  and then man  beneath mankind came the animals with  kingly animals like lions ranking above  the lowly creatures that crawled across  the sea floor  then came the plants also subdivided  into superior flowering plants  and inferior mushrooms and mosses  finally at the very bottom of the chain  came the minerals  which possessed none of the vitality of  life and yet still prove themselves of  value through their strength  and solidity this strict linear  classification is far removed from our  modern understanding of the living world  but nevertheless it was a world view  that persisted into the 19th century  it became the central canon for  pseudosciences like alchemy  since the linking of all matter in a  chain implied unity  and the theoretical possibility of  transmutation up that chain  to produce more kingly materials like  gold and diamond  early evolutionary scientists reasoned  that this transmutation was also  possible within living things  they believed that over time living  matter would transform  to become more advanced and move up the  great chain of being  this gave rise to the popular concept of  a missing link  [Music]  it may be theoretically possible for a  mere animal to become a man  but that link in the chain is hidden  from us  for now  [Music]  but there was a light at the end of the  tunnel for this seemingly unbreakable  chain of existence  [Music]  in the 18th century a swedish botanist  named carl linnaeus  proposed a new way of classifying life  he recognized the differences between  animals plants and minerals  but placed them in separate unrelated  groups  these groups he named kingdoms  ultimately creating similar unranked  subdivisions within them  they became classes orders genera and  species  every living thing therefore belonged in  a nested group of categories  that had no place for concepts of  biological superiority  thus for the first time humans could be  found within the kingdom of animals  the class of mammals and the order of  primates  their unique classification as humans  within the larger group of primates  is given by their binomial name  homo sapiens  [Music]  during this great age of scientific  discovery in the mid-1700s  carl linnaeus classified around 10 000  living things in this way  including six thousand plants and around  four thousand animals  [Music]  his meticulous organization laid the  foundation for all  modern biological classification and  binomial nomenclature has found its way  into common parlance  with the likes of tyrannosaurus rex and  escherichia coli  better known as the bacteria behind food  poisoning  e coli  today of course we have abandoned the  kingdom of minerals from biological  classification systems  three centuries of scientific discovery  have added other kingdoms alongside the  plant and animals  recognizing their fundamental and  irreconcilable differences  fungi and protists each earn their own  kingdom  and the inclusion of the prokaryotic  bacteria bring the total  to five  [Music]  but this isn't the full picture  just as the last three centuries or so  have brought new understanding of the  macroscopic world  so the last few decades have changed our  thinking on the micro cosmos too  the physical differences between  microscopic organisms may not be  immediately obvious  but as scientists probe even further  into their minute physiology and their  genetic makeup  even the simplest prokaryotes seem to be  remarkably diverse  of the one and a half million species  that have been formally described  prokaryotes make up less than one  percent  but scientists have good reason to  believe that there are many  many more still waiting to be discovered  [Music]  in 2012 about 1400 new bacterial species  previously unknown to science  were discovered living in the belly  buttons of university students  [Music]  the most recent estimates suggest that  the four other kingdoms of life  animals plants fungi and protists make  up just  20 per cent of all biodiversity  the rest numbering some one and a half  billion  species is all bacteria  [Music]  not only that but the divisions within  the prokaryotes are wider than we could  ever have imagined  although they may look similar to our  magnified eyes there are in fact  prokaryotes more distinct from one  another than bacteria  are from us  [Music]  in 1977 a pair of american  microbiologists  carl woes and george fox identified  fundamental differences in the genetic  structure of different types of simple  prokaryotes  these differences were so deep rooted  that they warranted not just a new  kingdom  but an entirely new level of  classification  this revelation changed the linear dogma  that had held sway  for 300 years  the exotic prokaryotes that had caused  such a stir became known as the archaea  and they define a domain of life  characterized by their distinctive  genetic makeup  archaean prokaryotes tend to live in  extreme environments  like hot springs intensely salty waters  and even inside petroleum deposits deep  underground  unlike their distant bacterial cousins  they never cause disease  with the recent discovery of the archaea  the new system of biological  classification recognizes  two other distinct domains  the true bacteria and the  nuclear-sparing eukaryotes  which include the animals plants fungi  and protist kingdoms  surprisingly when geneticists  reconstruct the tree of life  they find that we as members of the  eukaryotic domain are more closely  related to the extreme  archaea than we are to the bacteria that  live within our bodies  tracing our origins back in time  scientists believe that the eukaryotic  domain split from the archaea  around 2 billion years ago when the  endosymbiotic associations between  predatory prokaryotes and their  internally enslaved prey  became irreversible but the split  between bacterial  and archaeal branches is almost twice as  old  and may have occurred very soon after  the origin of life itself  and so we can picture the immense tree  of life  at 4 billion years old  [Music]  its colossal trunk meets the ground at a  single point  but soon diverges into two massive  branches  one representing the bacteria strikes  out for the sun  but the other splits in two again  these become the archaeal and eukaryotic  branches and each splits time and time  again  into dense crowns that represent not  only the huge diversity of life in  earth's modern biosphere  but every living being we know of that  has ever existed  [Music]  today homo sapiens as members of the  primate order  the mammalian class the vertebrate  phylum  the animal kingdom and the eukaryotic  domain  sit at the top of this elaborate and  incredibly complex tree  we may look down from the crown and  consider ourselves masters of all  but in reality we are merely a single  leaf sharing the sunlight  with billions of others all species  alive today can trace their heritage  down through the vast tree of life  to one of the three major branches and  then  to its root  ultimately we have the four billion year  long growth of this tree  to thank for our tenuous place  in the sun  you've been watching the entire history  of the earth don't forget to like and  subscribe  let us know what you think in the  comments and we'll see you  next time  you
 

